## 2026-06-25 — op-graph trie-native executor (execT) + optimizer fixes

Goal: report the RecursiveOpGraph's exec analog vs the interpreters, with the graph optimizer
(optimize_sharing CSE + push_out LICM/const-hoist) applied — and fix the optimizer first, since
it was not to be trusted.

Optimizer bugs found and fixed (MORKL.scala):
- Constant round-trip was LOSSY. transpile encoded path constants via `pi.show` and exec/execT
  re-parsed; but `show` of the empty path is "" (parses back to [Symbol("")]) and Arity items
  don't round-trip. n-queens place(6) produced 12456 paths (with a stray `..` empty item) vs
  eval's 144. Fixed with LiteralCodec.encodeConst/decodeConst: emit the readable `show` only
  when it parses back identically, else a base64 marker. After: eval=exec=execT=144.
- push_out double-added a stack level for a subgraph operand then the recursive call appended a
  second dead frame -> ArrayIndexOutOfBounds on sliding. Fixed with a `grewHere` guard
  (conditional addOne/dropRightInPlace).
- Residual: push_out still emits a malformed downward coordinate on very deep nesting (the
  9-level sliding iterN graph), crashing optimize_sharing. NOT fully fixed (deep rabbit hole).
  Instead made `optimize` TOTAL/SOUND: wellFormed(g) validates every input coordinate, and a
  `safe(pass)` wrapper runs each pass, keeps its output only if still well-formed (else identity).
  So optimize never corrupts a graph; it optimizes where valid and is the identity on the
  pathological case. All GraphExecT tests pass (incl. the sliding stress test).

New code:
- GraphExec.scala: execT — trie-native op-graph executor. Space slots hold ITrie, path slots
  hold interned List[Int]; NO intermediate eval, NO PathValue alloc (the old exec rebuilds
  Space.Literal and calls eval per node). Iteration iterates the source ITrie's children
  (IntMap) directly. runGraphT/runGraph fill Extract slots BY NAME (robust to optimizer reorder).
- GraphExecTest.scala (7 tests, all green): execT(transpile)==eval and optimize-preserves on
  aunt, n-queens(6), temperature, union_iter (asserts optimize actually CHANGES the graph), and
  the sliding expandStep stress test.
- GraphBench.scala (Slow): evalI vs exec vs execT vs execT(opt); all four asserted == ref.

Op-graph backend benchmark (best-of-N ms; full table in BENCHMARKS.md):
  program            evalI    exec   execT  execT(opt)  execT/exec
  aunt n=150           1.7    13.7     1.2      0.7        11.9x
  aunt n=400           2.8    83.4     2.2      2.0        37.4x
  n-queens n=6         6.9   134.4   137.3    124.5         1.0x
  n-queens n=7        18.5  1296.7  1926.9   1076.0         0.7x
  temperature 4096     0.0     7.5    21.3      7.0         0.4x
  temperature 16384    0.0    46.3    77.9     38.5         0.6x
  union_iter           0.1     1.0     0.1      0.1         6.9x

Reading: execT crushes exec on relational/iter-heavy work (aunt 12-37x, union_iter 7x) where the
trie's structural merges replace per-node Set rebuilds. On n-queens and temperature, execT over
the RAW graph is SLOWER than exec because the loop body re-decodes/re-interns Literal constants
every iteration; the OPTIMIZER (const-hoisting) recovers it — n-queens n=7 1927->1076ms, temp
4096 21->7ms, temp 16384 78->38ms — i.e. optimize's main win is exactly hoisting the literal
table builds out of the loops. evalI still dominates all graph executors (the op-graph adds
stack-frame and Call overhead the direct evaluator avoids); the graph path's value is a compiled,
optimizable IR, not raw speed over the interpreter.

