## 2026-06-25 (cont.) — push_out fixed, Call inlining, recursion lowering, exec ≥ eval

User: "Fix the bug in push_out and while being passed into the graph executor, all functions have
to be inlined (expanded into the graph) and lowered (the union saturating recursion). There's no
reason for exec to be slower than eval."

1. push_out (LICM) REWRITTEN and fixed (MORKL.scala). The old version threaded one mutable
   level->coordinate stack through a gather/recurse pair; on deep nesting the deeper level-maps were
   reused across sibling/ancestor scopes, so a node that wasn't re-hoisted at the inner level kept a
   coordinate pointing into the wrong graph (the malformed downward coord (11,3) on sliding 3x3).
   New algorithm is coordinate-free until the end: every node gets a stable id; inputs resolve to
   ids; each node's earliest-legal scope = deepest scope among its inputs' placements (constants ->
   root; Extracts & each scope's result node pinned; subgraphs pinned — move nodes, never scopes);
   a final top-down pass lays each scope out [pinned Extracts at slots 0..k-1] ++ [interior topo] ++
   [result last] and assigns fresh coords. Well-formed BY CONSTRUCTION at any depth. Two correctness
   points found via tests: (a) a scope's result can itself be an Extract (identity-body iteration) —
   exclude it from the extract list to avoid duplication; (b) a subgraph must be ordered after EVERY
   same-scope node referenced anywhere in its body subtree (not just its root.inputs) — else a
   hoisted constant lands after the iteration that uses it and the executor reads a null slot
   (the union_iter "xs is null" crash). Fixed via subtreeRefs (allRefs computed bottom-up).
   `optimize`'s safe/wellFormed guard now never triggers (kept as cheap defence).

2. Constant decoding cached (IntTrie.scala iLiteralStr/internConstStr, used by execT). The op-graph
   stores Literal/Constant payloads as STRINGS; execT was re-decoding (base64+parse+intern) and
   rebuilding the trie on EVERY node execution (iLiteral's identity-memo always missed on the fresh
   SpaceValue). Now each distinct constant string decodes once process-wide; thereafter one O(1)
   lookup. This alone cut execT(transpile): n-queens n=7 1927->147ms, temperature 16384 78->0.1ms.

3. Call inlining (MORKL.scala inlineCalls) — source-level fixpoint of Lower.inline: every Call to a
   non-recursive routine is spliced in (args for params), so transpile yields a Call-FREE graph and
   the executor needs no dispatch. Verified: n-queens place(6) and sliding 3x3 expandStep both go to
   0 Call nodes and execT(inline+opt) == eval.

4. Union-saturating recursion LOWERED to a Fixpoint subgraph (transpile). Pattern r(m) = m \/ r(next(m))
   (single mention, extensive/monotone next: the datalog shape) -> a Fixpoint subgraph whose body is
   next(cur); exec/execT iterate cur := next(cur) to the least fixpoint (ITrie == is structural).
   transitive_routine now transpiles Call-free; execT(Fixpoint)==exec==eval, and optimize preserves
   it. (Multi-mention recursion — reachable/scc — is not yet matched; falls back to a Call node.)

Op-graph benchmark (best-of-N ms; full table in BENCHMARKS.md). execT(inline+opt) is the
executor-ready form; last column is execT(inline+opt)/evalI (<1 = compiled graph beats interpreter):
  program          evalI   exec   execT  execT(opt)  inline+opt  vs evalI
  aunt n=400         1.7  120.6    1.4     1.3         1.2        0.72
  n-queens n=6       7.1  118.0   33.5    29.1         0.9        0.13
  n-queens n=7       9.3  564.2  146.9   142.8         3.6        0.39
  union_iter         0.0    0.5    0.0     0.0         0.0        0.48
  datalog tc n=40    1.7    —      1.1     1.0         1.0        0.57   (Fixpoint-lowered)
  datalog tc n=80   17.6    —     11.8    11.9        11.9        0.68
execT(inline+opt) now BEATS evalI on every non-trivial program (n-queens up to ~8x, datalog ~1.5x);
the eval-based `exec` (rebuilds Literals + calls eval per node) is the slow one it replaces.
Tests: push_out depth 1..4 + sliding, inline Call-free, datalog->Fixpoint, all in GraphExecTest.

