[2026-06-27] Streaming cursor for zipper Iteration — TRIED, did NOT help, reverted.
Implemented an n-ary union cursor `UnionN` (one grouping pass per level = the joinAll shape, instead of
the earlier O(n log n) re-merging binary Union tree) and used it to combine the per-head fused bodies.
Result on royal92 aunt (3008 heads): execZ ~82 ms vs evalI 2.5 ms — essentially identical to the eager
binary-tree version (~83 ms). So the union combiner is NOT the bottleneck.
Root cause: the cost is materializing 3008 per-head fused-algebra bodies NODE-BY-NODE (the zipper's
constant-factor overhead) where evalI uses bulk Patricia ops (IntTrieOps) with empty short-circuits. With
no OUTER operator to prune the bodies, fusion has nothing to deforest, so evalI is strictly better.
Decision: Iteration stays on the evalI "call". Removed UnionN (dead once Iteration reverted). royal92 aunt
restored to parity: eval 6641 / evalI 2.12 / execT 1.50 / execZ 2.14 ms (1.01x evalI). Full suite 195/195.
A real Iteration win needs the byte/bit-trie under the symbols, or an outer-pruned iteration — not a
different union combiner.

